This Python function has a bug — it's supposed to flatten a nested dict into dot-separated keys:
```python
def flatten(d, prefix=''):
    items = {}
    for k, v in d.items():
        key = f'{prefix}.{k}' if prefix else k
        if isinstance(v, dict):
            items.update(flatten(v, key))
        else:
            items[key] = v
    return items
```
The bug: it infinite-loops on self-referencing dicts (e.g. d['x'] = d). Fix it so it detects cycles and replaces them with the string '<cycle>' instead of recursing infinitely. Also handle lists by indexing them (e.g. key.0, key.1). Output only the fixed function.

response.text:
```python
def flatten(d, prefix=''):
    items = {}
    seen = set()  # ids of containers on the current recursion path, for cycle detection

    def walk(value, key):
        if isinstance(value, (dict, list)):
            if id(value) in seen:          # back-reference -> cycle
                items[key] = '<cycle>'
                return
            seen.add(id(value))
            pairs = value.items() if isinstance(value, dict) else enumerate(value)
            for k, v in pairs:
                walk(v, f'{key}.{k}' if key else k)
            seen.discard(id(value))        # allow the same dict in sibling branches (DAGs)
        else:
            items[key] = value

    walk(d, prefix)
    return items
```
